AYe0OvDld+OQBnlVx19D7fv7H8Yg/w/gEEtG2vOsPX/a4JBX/YrtGY6Y2BHf
mTjEwA2/oieWLXbsAzOtpYHYw9DNtjik2a6fVh+0Wvr20l5nB/9GHMH5iNZG
E4dYbX8dDml6PQ5p0Tkio57NqGPDLz874BADR7S8gkOMvK/gkKbX45AG2vg2
cupY37b91xE2qPsZOIT93mziEM5h2NpNHPKLiUPqHPTCEYe8kN+FQxp+A4f8
9O/2PI44pL08Kg5pUL42vuD42NHGOIyzLTtZX2fIhjWv42DDKS/0ly0Nli19
FYe0NDhgDcse23BIvTj6UPpIA4c4yrU5l9TGL6K/G18aWPK1OMQBS78xDrHq
V6+6oTJm0wN7/e04xCzTZOGedn6j0VF3X9r7wVE/2vdpi4VDflGdaLFscZv8
ZvkOx3Idj0fsOMTRX7YvZ8egdn63t8/tcIheO4x/W94Uh5jPbWMn2vGno7nz
V3BIB/EO877GvEOdff7YNh/U6NDWjnDIm+LWdmUsHXkFh7TLY8Mhlg68IQ5x
5M9/GQ4BSWPH1NrYQVstfSUOqVcb0kqd/C0c8vb39vf29/b39vf29/b39vf2
9/b39vf29/b39vf29/b3/8Hv/wBNKpWh
     "], {{0, 215.}, {273., 0}}, {0, 255},
     ColorFunction->RGBColor,
     ImageResolution->{144., 144.},
     RasterInterpolation->"High"],
    BoxForm`ImageTag["Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True],
    Selectable->False],
   DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
   ImageSize->Automatic,
   ImageSizeRaw->{273., 215.},
   PlotRange->{{0, 273.}, {0, 215.}}]], "Input",ExpressionUUID->
  "3fc38cfa-a90c-4068-bc07-11858b1d2892"],
 "\nHarold Black invented the negative feedback amplifier in 1927 at Bell \
Labs.\n\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]He paved the way for routine long-distance \
telephone service by eliminating distortions that could reduce to gibberish \
the sound of a phone call amplified over and over again on its way across the \
country.\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] From http://www.alcatel-lucent.com"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9687531118114862`*^9, 3.9687531792793694`*^9}, 
   3.9861176642925863`*^9},
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"dc7a2a50-863d-4151-959a-6e59e1434174"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Ideal op amps have an infinite gain. In practice the open-loop gain is \
typically in range of  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "6"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "50639b65-0482-457a-b100-5821aa6e87c2"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "7"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "c4b37aa6-508c-4220-9343-195ffbadc26e"],
 ". In an ideal op amp no current flows in to either input (i.e. they are \
entirely voltage controlled and have infinite input impedance). In practice \
the input impedances are typically in the  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "8"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f79f6db2-0089-4a99-a02c-2fa2800666dd"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "12"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "1f60a708-3bd5-4586-a148-9bd09c84776f"],
 " \[CapitalOmega] range and have offset and bias currents in the nA range \
(in very special purpose op amps the input and bias currents can be in the fA \
range). Ideal op amps act as pure voltage sources (i.e., they can be modeled \
as a Thevenin source with very low output impedance). In reality op amps \
typically have open-loop output impedances in the \[CapitalOmega] range (and \
closed-loop output impedances in the m\[CapitalOmega] range) and output \
offsets (due to a variety of internal design factors) in the 10 \[Mu]V to 1 \
mV range (the output offset voltage also drifts with temperature changes). \
Most op amps provide offset trimming capabilities to null the output (set it \
to zero for zero input). \n\nOp amps can typically function using a range of \
supply voltages (e.g., \[PlusMinus]5 V to \[PlusMinus]18 V depending on the \
type). The input voltages can usually go to within 2 V of the supply voltage. \
Indeed some op amps can operate from a single supply voltage. The output of \
common op amps can only drive low currents (e.g., 10 mA). However special \
purpose high current output op amps (called power op amps) are available with \
output drive capabilities to 50 A. Another class of special purpose op amps \
is available for high voltage applications (e.g., driving piezoelectric \
actuators) and can deliver up to \[PlusMinus]1500 V."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968753203021839*^9, 3.968753268953931*^9}, {
  3.96875364855198*^9, 3.9687536787826023`*^9}, {3.968770674678281*^9, 
  3.968770706835569*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8d898464-4e7e-4862-825f-\
878f1fcde25d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Ideal op amps have an infinite open-loop bandwidth. In practice the \
closed-loop bandwidth is dependent on the feedback gain (the higher the gain \
the lower the bandwidth). For this reason the op amp data sheet will always \
specify its gain bandwidth product (e.g., 1 MHz (i.e. for a gain of 10 the \
bandwidth would extend from 0 to 100 kHz)). \n\nAlthough op amps may be \
considered to be dynamically linear devices under many conditions they do \
display some nonlinear behavior. The major nonlinearity is an op amps \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]slew rate\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] limit. This is the \
maximum achievable change in output voltage per unit time. Typical precision \
op amps have slew-rate limits in the order of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "6"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "caaeac59-42ca-4d6d-99cd-90d1e4f4994a"],
 "  V/s . Op amps used in high performance video systems need to achieve much \
higher slew-rates. The slew-rate limit is the reason why the bandwidth of an \
op amp is wider for small signals than large. For this reason it is common \
for op amp spec sheets to specify both the small signal and large signal \
responses. \n\nThe degree to which an op amp is able to perform the \
subtraction of the inverting input from the non-inverting input is measured \
by its common mode rejection ratio (CMRR). CMRR is discussed in detail in a \
subsequent section on instrumentation amplifiers. A typical modern op amp \
will have a common mode rejection ratio in the order of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "6"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "6d2af090-c910-44bd-83f2-d2d4d4ad155e"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", "7"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "c3825c0e-1584-4ad7-b583-544d06a1aa22"],
 ". \n\nAnother important op amp specification is the power supply rejection \
ratio (PSRR). Early op amps had very little ability to reject power supply \
variations (e.g., as caused by batteries running down). Modern op amps are \
typically able to attenuate the effect of power supply changes by as much as \
10",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["", "7"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "b43c5c1a-f9c5-4d68-93a1-2feaae7b99a2"],
 " times  (10",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["", "5"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "13b003db-494e-4ca5-b20b-257fcb8aae1f"],
 " typical). The op amp spec sheet usually specifies both the CMRR and PSRR \
in dB (e.g., 10",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["", "7"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "22ef1eb0-d98c-44ba-b69a-44e0e40ab495"],
 " is 140 dB). \n\nModern op amps are low noise devices. The total output \
voltage noise increases with bandwidth (for this reason you should limit the \
bandwidth of an op amp to the frequency range of interest). Op amp spec \
sheets usually specify a peak-to-peak voltage noise at low frequencies and a \
noise density (in V/",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SqrtBox["Hz"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "c55e9247-ebe1-4725-8415-e3f4ae73a603"],
 ") at higher bandwidths. \n\nThere are 1000s of different op amps available. \
One of the first popular op amps in chip form was (and is) called the 741 and \
is usually sold as a chip with 8 legs (called an 8 pin DIP (dual inline \
package)). Many op amps follow the pin out of the original 741 op amp which \
is shown on the left below. Many modern op amps use a slightly different \
pin-out as shown below right. If output nulling is not required then the \
offset trim pins are simply left disconnected. Unlike the classic 741 many \
modern op amps will survive a brief accidental inversion of the supply \
voltage polarity."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968753287278322*^9, 3.968753570241418*^9}, {
  3.968770843288836*^9, 
  3.968770893147243*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"887b276a-a375-414f-93de-\
64c6cd7a240a"],

