Cell["\<\
These are amazingly close to the desired values! 
The resistor values used in the circuit are type metal film with 0.1% \
resistance tolerance and the capacitors are type ceramic with 1% capacitance \
tolerance. These are expensive components and result in circuits with less \
variation in \[Alpha], \[Omega], and \[Zeta] than usual.
For reference: metal film resistors are normally within around 1% and ceramic \
capacitors are normally within 5%.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.795811818037542*^9, 3.7958118278046455`*^9}, {
  3.950203224316099*^9, 3.9502033196146393`*^9}, {3.9698134629854803`*^9, 
  3.969813657461104*^9}, {3.986132066777789*^9, 
  3.986132071259118*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fd233af8-c098-42b6-aad5-\
3535d9033f9c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Current to Voltage Converter", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9687718717759943`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"fe69eb56-5f71-42b1-b65c-\
bd41853432e2"],

Cell["\<\
It is often the case that a sensor produces a current rather than a voltage. \
For example photo-diodes produce a current which is proportional to the \
incident light intensity. The following circuit converts this current in to a \
voltage.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968771883084859*^9, 
  3.968771897520378*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a7311992-0137-4737-b150-\
989a6dba0688"],

