Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Specialty: the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]indirect current feedback\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] architecture", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.986124086301834*^9},ExpressionUUID->"de3dae06-f64f-4b6c-a0f7-\
b856751bd3b0"],

Cell["\<\
These deserve a separate mention because they break the gain\[Dash]CMRR \
tradeoff inherent in the classical three-op-amp INA topology. Worth knowing \
about:

\t\[Bullet]\tAD8295 \[LongDash] INA + two auxiliary op amps + matched \
resistors in one package (signal \t\t\t\tconditioning subsystem)
\t\[Bullet]\tAD8237 \[LongDash] micropower indirect current feedback, \
rail-to-rail input and output
\t\[Bullet]\tMAX4194 / MAX4197 \[LongDash] micropower, pin-compatible \
alternatives in the AD620 footprint\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.986124108641954*^9, {3.9861241400953703`*^9, 
   3.9861241712771397`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"33f08c3d-bfb7-45b2-bc09-\
35a0c5977e7d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Practical recommendations for the Typical Labs", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986124205702922*^9, 
  3.986124216183771*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"afa4f2ff-d255-4871-b361-\
dc8ea093fcb1"],

Cell["\<\
If I were stocking a single drawer, the parts that give 90% coverage are:

\t\[Bullet]\tOPA627 for high-Z FET-input precision work (photodiode amps, \
electrometer front-ends)
\t\[Bullet]\tOP27 / OPA211 for low-noise sensor preamps with bipolar inputs
\t\[Bullet]\tLTC2057 when DC accuracy matters more than bandwidth
\t\[Bullet]\tAD797 for the rare combination of low noise and high speed
\t\[Bullet]\tTL072 / OPA1642 for everyday bread boarding
\t\[Bullet]\tAD8221 or INA128 as the default INA
\t\[Bullet]\tAD8429 when input-referred noise matters (electrophysiology, \
low-frequency strain gauges)
\t\[Bullet]\tLTC2053 when DC accuracy in an INA matters (thermocouples, \
long-term drift)\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.98612424285777*^9, 3.986124294027338*^9}, {
  3.9861243293881063`*^9, 
  3.986124358430478*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9040bc52-f94f-4508-ae62-\
958f2920af40"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Power Buffers", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986117783476858*^9, 3.98611778661273*^9}, {
  3.98611984359341*^9, 
  3.986119844935932*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cff531aa-706d-4aab-ab2f-\
14b0d3666cb0"],

Cell["\<\
All the op amp circuits we have looked at so far would typically be built \
using op amps with limited current driving capabilities (typically around \
\[PlusMinus]20 mA continuous and \[PlusMinus]35 mA max). This is insufficient \
to drive motors, speakers, and lights (except for LEDs requiring <20 mA). \
There are two approaches you can take if you need more current. 
The first is to use a fast unity-gain buffer such as a LT1010 (Linear \
Technology, now Analog Devices) or equivalent which is designed to be placed \
inside the feedback loop of an op amp to extend its output current and speed \
without disturbing the loop dynamics. The LT1010 can drive \[PlusMinus]150 mA \
continuous and \[PlusMinus]200 mA max. 
For new designs that need more current or higher speed, you would now reach \
for:

\t\[Bullet]\tBUF634A (TI, 250 mA, 210 MHz) \[LongDash] closest direct \
replacement, with selectable bandwidth via a bias pin (same idea as LT1010).
\t\[Bullet]\tLT1206 / LT1210 (250 mA / 1.1 A current-feedback amplifiers) \
\[LongDash] when you also want gain.

These power buffers can be used inside the feedback loop of both the \
inverting and non-inverting op amp configurations shown earlier.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986117798924758*^9, 3.986117888052362*^9}, {
  3.986118477854569*^9, 3.986118578960779*^9}, {3.986118614368575*^9, 
  3.986118796777487*^9}, {3.986119223067627*^9, 3.986119337021945*^9}, {
  3.986119368364777*^9, 3.9861193946456413`*^9}, {3.986119782729824*^9, 
  3.986119816802197*^9}, {3.9861199067213163`*^9, 3.986119927909656*^9}, {
